Z||000000||Smokin87||03-22-2001||08:35 AM||||Where is the screw located on the caliper to bleed the brakes.  Is it obvious, and do i need a special screw driver?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_copy.jpg">  <BR>"When man wanted to make the car faster...he invented the jet-plane."||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||03-22-2001||08:51 AM||||It is obvious.<P>You're worrying me with these questions. Brakes are important, I just want to make sure you know what you're doing, and that your brakes will work when needed after the work.<P>Don't you have a shop manual or anything?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Smokin87||03-22-2001||09:01 AM||||No, it cool, <BR>Ive done brakes before, ive done a full job, pads, calipers and roters.  Its just that i did it on a big truck, i didnt have to take the wheels off, i just rolled under it.  I thought it would be pretty obvious, but i just wanted to have an idea of what to look for before i do it. Becides all im doing is replacing the master cylinder, that is just 2 bolts and the lines, then i just have to bleed the brakes and add some fluid.  Piece of kake.||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||03-22-2001||09:19 AM||||heh, sometimes it's easier said than done. Remember that sometimes it's required to bleed the MC before you install it. I was working on the MC for an 88 S-10 once which turned out to be a whole lot more trouble than I'd anticipated. Especially since I went through about 5 rebuilt cylinders before I got one that was rebuilt correctly and didn't have an internal leak. BTW, the bleeder screw it typically located near the top of the caliper, usually above the brake line.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||03-22-2001||09:20 AM||||If I remember right, you need a common 1/4" combination wrench to loosen the bleeder screws (or was it 10 mm?), and the screws are on the top of each caliper, facing up.<P>Incidentally, I have never had any luck with the one-man bleeders...there's always bubbles in the fluid unless I use the tried-and-true "buddy pushes pedal down while I loosen and tighten screws" method.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smokin87||03-22-2001||10:19 PM||||Ya, i found them, they are 10mm, but i got so pissed, because the last guy to do the brakes totally stripped the front 2 bleeder valves, now i have to wrench them out...FUN!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||03-23-2001||06:27 PM||||Replace them and avoid the hassle next time...||||reg||1
